1. Customer Data Plane (AWS Account)





Databricks compute resources (clusters) are deployed within the customer-managed AWS VPC.



Clusters run in private subnets (Connected Local Subnets) across multiple availability zones.
These clusters process data but do not have direct internet exposure.
All outbound communication is routed through controlled network paths.




👉 This ensures:



Data remains in the customer account
Full control over networking and security boundaries










🔐 2. Private Connectivity via VPC Endpoints (PrivateLink)





Clusters communicate with both AWS services and Databricks control plane using VPC endpoints (PrivateLink).





Two types of endpoints are used:







a. AWS Service Endpoints





Provide private access to:



Amazon S3 (data storage)
AWS Glue (metadata/catalog integration)
Kinesis and other services






b. Databricks Control Plane Endpoints





Provide private communication to:



Databricks REST APIs
Secure Cluster Connectivity (SCC)




👉 This means:



No traffic traverses the public internet
All communication stays within AWS private networking










🔄 3. Secure Cluster Connectivity (SCC)





Clusters establish outbound-only secure connections to the Databricks control plane.



No inbound access to clusters is required
Control plane interactions (job execution, commands, monitoring) occur through this secure channel




👉 This enforces a zero inbound access model for compute resources









🧠 4. Databricks Control Plane (Databricks Account)





The control plane is fully managed by Databricks and operates outside the customer account.



It is responsible for:



Workspace UI and APIs
Job orchestration and scheduling
Cluster lifecycle management (create, scale, terminate)
Unity Catalog governance (permissions, lineage, access control)




Communication between data plane and control plane occurs via private VPC endpoints.









🔑 5. Cross-Account IAM Access





Databricks uses cross-account IAM roles to securely interact with customer resources.





Roles include:





Cluster provisioning role
→ Allows Databricks to create and manage compute resources
Catalog access role
→ Enables access to governed data in S3 and external locations




👉 Access is:



Temporary (via STS assume role)
Controlled by least privilege policies










🗄️ 6. Data Access and Storage





Data remains fully within the customer AWS account:



Workspace Root S3 Bucket
→ Stores logs, artifacts, and workspace-level data
External Locations (Unity Catalog)
→ Governed access to enterprise datasets




👉 All data access is:



Governed through Unity Catalog
Enforced via IAM roles and policies










🛡️ 7. Network Security Controls





Additional security mechanisms include:



Restrictive VPC Endpoint Policies
→ Limit which services and endpoints can be accessed
Private subnets only
→ No direct internet routing
Separation of control and data planes
→ Reduces blast radius and enforces isolation










👤 8. Identity and Access Integration





User authentication and authorization are handled via enterprise identity systems:



Databricks integrates with Ping Identity → Active Directory
Users, groups, and service principals are synchronized into the workspace
Access to data is enforced via Unity Catalog ACLs
